-- title:
--   Smooth Drinfeld charts at a supersingular point, q=3
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $q$ is a prime with $q = 3$ and $M'$ is a nonzero natural number not divisible by $q$; in addition a prime $\ell$ with $\ell \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$ and $\ell \mid M'$ is fixed (a rigidifying auxiliary level).
--
--   The geometric data. $A$ is a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}} =$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ` with `A.LiesOverPrime q`, i.e. $q$ lies in the nonunits of $A$. $W$ is a finite set of places of $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC}(\mathrm{ResidueField}\,A)\,M'$ over $\mathrm{ResidueField}\,A$ — the subfield of Laurent series generated by the $j$-series and its $M'$-th pullback — and the hypothesis `hW` says that $W$ consists exactly of the supersingular places `ssPlaces q M' (ResidueField A)`, i.e. the places $w$ that are rational, satisfy `IsAffineGeomPlace`, and whose value at the geometric $j$-generator lies in `ssJSet q`. Here and below $\mathrm{fieldBar}\,q\,M'$ is the base change to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of the $X_H$-function field of level $q^2M'$, $H$ being the kernel of $(\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/q)^\times$, and $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\,M'$ is the base change to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of the full level-$M'$ modular function field; `hle` asserts the inclusion of the latter in the former. $R_0$ is a `ConstantReduction` of $A$ on $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\,M'$ with values in $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC}(\mathrm{ResidueField}\,A)\,M'$: a valuation subring `R₀.integers` whose intersection with the constants is $A$, a surjective residue homomorphism onto the target field with kernel the maximal ideal and compatible with reduction of constants, together with a degree- and divisor-compatible map on places. The hypothesis `hR₀` says that for a Laurent series $y$ with coefficients in $A$ whose image in the Laurent series over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ lies in $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\,M'$, this element lies in `R₀.integers` and its $R_0$-residue is the coefficientwise reduction of $y$ modulo the maximal ideal of $A$. Further, $\pi \in \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ satisfies $\pi^{q^2-1} = q$ and $\pi \in A$, and $\zeta$ is a primitive $q$-th root of unity in $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ (an element of `Idx q`).
--
--   The valuation subrings of the components. $O_{\mathrm{Ig}}$ assigns a valuation subring of $\mathrm{fieldBar}\,q\,M'$ to each point of $\mathbb{P}^1(\mathbb{Z}/q)$ and $O_{\mathrm{SS}}$ one to each $s \in W$. Four hypotheses describe $O_{\mathrm{Ig}}$: `hIg_inf` says that $f$ lies in $O_{\mathrm{Ig}}$ at the line `lineInfty q` $= [1:0]$ precisely when there are Laurent series $x, y$ with coefficients in $A$ such that the coefficientwise reduction of $y$ is nonzero and $f \cdot y = x$ as Laurent series over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$; `hIg` says that every line $L$ is $\mathrm{redQ}\,q\,\gamma \cdot [1:0]$ for some $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ with $O_{\mathrm{Ig}}(L)$ the preimage of $O_{\mathrm{Ig}}([1:0])$ under the level automorphism $\mathrm{levelAutBar}\,q\,M'\,\zeta\,\gamma$; `hIg_inj` says $O_{\mathrm{Ig}}$ is injective; `hIg_perm` says that for every primitive root $\zeta'$ and every $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ the operation of taking preimages under $\mathrm{levelAutBar}\,q\,M'\,\zeta'\,\gamma$ permutes the family $O_{\mathrm{Ig}}$. Three hypotheses describe $O_{\mathrm{SS}}$: `hSS_A` says that the constants in $O_{\mathrm{SS}}(s)$ are exactly $A$, for every $s$; `hSS_over` says that if $f \in \mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\,M'$ lies in `R₀.integers`, has nonnegative order at every place of $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\,M'$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ at which the $j$-series $\hat{j}$ (the image of `jq` under coefficient extension) has nonnegative order, and has $R_0$-residue inside the valuation subring of the place $s$, then the image of $f$ in $\mathrm{fieldBar}\,q\,M'$ lies in $O_{\mathrm{SS}}(s)$ and, for every $a \in A$ whose residue equals the value of the $R_0$-residue of $f$ at $s$, the difference of the image of $f$ and the image of $a$ lies in $O_{\mathrm{SS}}(s)$ and in its maximal ideal; `hSS_fix` says each $O_{\mathrm{SS}}(s)$ is its own preimage under every $\mathrm{levelAutBar}\,q\,M'\,\zeta'\,\gamma$ with $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$; `hSS_tr` provides, for each $s$, an element $t \in O_{\mathrm{SS}}(s)$ such that $t - a$ lies in $O_{\mathrm{SS}}(s)$ and is a unit there for every $a \in A$.
--
--   The descent data. $K_0$ is a subfield of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ over which $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ is algebraic and which contains $\pi$. $A_0$ is a Henselian discrete valuation ring that is a domain, equipped with an injective local ring homomorphism $\iota : A_0 \to A$ whose image, viewed inside $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, is exactly $A \cap K_0$ (`hιK₀`), such that the composite of $\iota$ with the residue map of $A$ is surjective (`hres`), with a uniformiser $\varpi_0$ generating the maximal ideal (`hϖ₀`) and $\iota(\varpi_0) = \pi$ (`hϖ₀π`). $F_0$ is a subfield of $\mathrm{fieldBar}\,q\,M'$, characterised by `hF₀` as the set of elements all of whose Laurent coefficients lie in $K_0$; `hjF₀` asserts that $\hat{j}$ lies in $F_0$, and the $A_0$-algebra structure on $F_0$ is pinned by `hj₀` to be $\iota$ followed by the inclusion of the constants $\overline{\mathbb{Q}} \to \mathrm{fieldBar}\,q\,M'$.
--
--   The model. $X_0$ is a scheme with a morphism $\mathrm{toBase}_0 : X_0 \to \operatorname{Spec} A_0$ that is proper, flat and locally of finite presentation, $X_0$ being integral, all of whose stalks are integrally closed (`hn₀`); $\varphi_0$ is a ring isomorphism of $F_0$ with the function field of $X_0$ compatible with the structural map $A_0 \to \mathcal{K}(X_0)$ (`hφ₀`). The hypothesis `hdim` expresses relative dimension one: if $\eta$ lies over the closed point of $A_0$ and is not closed (it specialises to some $z \neq \eta$), then every $y \neq \eta$ to which $\eta$ specialises is a closed point. The component dictionary is a map $\mathrm{gen} : \mathbb{P}^1(\mathbb{Z}/q) \oplus W \to X_0$ with: `hgenIg`, the subring of $F_0$ traced out by the stalk at $\mathrm{gen}(\mathrm{inl}\,L)$ (via `SemistableModel.localRing`) consists of the elements whose image in $\mathrm{fieldBar}\,q\,M'$ lies in $O_{\mathrm{Ig}}(L)$; `hgenSS`, the same for $\mathrm{gen}(\mathrm{inr}\,s)$ and $O_{\mathrm{SS}}(s)$; `hgen₀`, every $\mathrm{gen}\,i$ lies over the closed point of $A_0$; `hgen`, a point of $X_0$ over the closed point is of the form $\mathrm{gen}\,i$ if and only if it is not closed. Finally $s \in W$ is fixed.
--
--   Conclusion. Two assertions hold.
--
--   First, $\mathrm{gen}(\mathrm{inr}\,s)$ lies in the smooth locus of $\mathrm{toBase}_0$.
--
--   Second, for every point $x_0$ of $X_0$ lying over the closed point of $A_0$ which is closed (every $y$ with $x_0 \rightsquigarrow y$ equals $x_0$), to which $\mathrm{gen}(\mathrm{inr}\,s)$ specialises, and which lies on no Igusa component (for no line $L$ does $\mathrm{gen}(\mathrm{inl}\,L)$ specialise to $x_0$), there exist an $A_0$-subalgebra $B$ of $F_0$ and a maximal ideal $\mathfrak{m}$ of $B$ such that all of the following hold: $B$ is a finitely generated $A_0$-algebra; $B$ is integrally closed in $F_0$, every $x \in F_0$ integral over $B$ lying in $B$; $F_0$ is the field of fractions of $B$, each $x \in F_0$ being of the form $b c^{-1}$ with $b, c \in B$, $c \neq 0$; every prime $\mathfrak{q}$ of $B$ containing the image of the maximal ideal of $A_0$ and not maximal is a minimal prime over that image; for every nonzero prime $\mathfrak{p}$ of $B$ not containing the image of the maximal ideal of $A_0$, the localisation $\{f \in F_0 : f c = b \text{ for some } b, c \in B,\ c \notin \mathfrak{p}\}$ is a valuation subring of $F_0$; for every non-closed point $\eta$ of $X_0$ over the closed point of $A_0$ whose traced local ring contains $B$, there is a prime $\mathfrak{q}$ of $B$ such that that local ring is the localisation of $B$ at $\mathfrak{q}$; conversely, every minimal prime $\mathfrak{q}$ over the image of the maximal ideal of $A_0$ arises in this way from some non-closed point $\eta$ over the closed point; the element $\hat{j}$ of $F_0$ furnished by `hjF₀` lies in $B$; for every index $i$, if $B$ is contained in the traced local ring at $\mathrm{gen}\,i$ then $i = \mathrm{inr}\,s$, so that the Drinfeld component at $s$ is the only component whose chart contains $B$; the traced local ring at $x_0$ is the localisation of $B$ at $\mathfrak{m}$, that is, consists of the $f \in F_0$ with $f c = b$ for some $b, c \in B$, $c \notin \mathfrak{m}$; and the ring homomorphism from $A_0$ to `Localization.AtPrime 𝔪` is formally smooth.
--
--   This is the $q = 3$ case, at a rigidified auxiliary level (a prime $\ell \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$ dividing $M'$), of the statement that the Drinfeld (supersingular) component of the special fibre of the descended full-level model is smooth at its generic point and that each of its closed points off the Igusa components lies in a normal finitely generated affine chart which meets no other component and is formally smooth after localisation. It is used in the construction of the semistable scheme over the descent base $A_0$ from the valuation-theoretic data $O_{\mathrm{Ig}}$, $O_{\mathrm{SS}}$, in [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.exists_semistableScheme_over_descentBase_of_valuationSubrings_of_eq_pi_relDimOne_jDich_of_eq_three_of_dvd`](thm.html#ModularCurve.FullLevel.exists_semistableScheme_over_descentBase_of_valuationSubrings_of_eq_pi_relDimOne_jDich_of_eq_three_of_dvd).
